WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.908 --> 00:00:06.087
- [Narrator] How great people think.

00:00:06.087 --> 00:00:07.734
On April 16, 2000,

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:10.053
the New England Patriots
drafted an extra quarterback

00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:11.329
out of Michigan State.

00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:12.666
They had scouted him thoroughly

00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:14.829
and had their eye on him for sometime.

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:16.981
Seeing that he was still
available, they took him.

00:00:16.981 --> 00:00:20.760
It was the sixth round and
the 199th pick of the draft.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.717
The young quarterback's
name was Tom Brady.

00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:26.459
He was fourth string at the
beginning of his rookie season.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.886
By his second season, he was a starter.

00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:30.988
New England won the Super Bowl that year.

00:00:30.988 --> 00:00:32.655
Brady was named MVP.

00:00:33.578 --> 00:00:35.083
In terms of return on investment,

00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:37.459
it's probably the single
greatest draft pick

00:00:37.459 --> 00:00:39.732
in the history of football.

00:00:39.732 --> 00:00:42.111
Four Super Bowl rings
out of six appearances,

00:00:42.111 --> 00:00:44.884
13 starting seasons, 160 wins,

00:00:44.884 --> 00:00:48.149
392 touchdowns, three Super Bowl MVPs,

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:50.781
53,000 yards, 10 pro balls,

00:00:50.781 --> 00:00:54.361
and more division titles than
any quarterback in history.

00:00:54.361 --> 00:00:56.334
It's not even finished paying dividends.

00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:59.435
Brady may still have many
more seasons left in him.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:00.845
So you'd think the Patriots' front office

00:01:00.845 --> 00:01:02.718
would be ecstatic with how it turned out

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:04.344
and indeed they were.

00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:05.849
They were also disappointed,

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:08.403
deeply so in themselves.

00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:09.885
Brady's surprising abilities

00:01:09.885 --> 00:01:13.098
meant that the Patriots'
scouting reports were way off

00:01:13.098 --> 00:01:14.972
for all their evaluations of players.

00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:17.381
They had somehow missed or miscalculated

00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:19.621
all of his intangible attributes.

00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:23.347
They had let this gem wait
until the sixth round.

00:01:23.347 --> 00:01:25.108
Someone else could have drafted him.

00:01:25.108 --> 00:01:25.941
More than that,

00:01:25.941 --> 00:01:28.018
they didn't even know they
were right about Brady

00:01:28.018 --> 00:01:30.647
until injuries knocked out Drew Bledsoe,

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:31.930
their prized starter,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.187
and forced them to realize his potential.

00:01:35.187 --> 00:01:36.823
So even though their bet paid off,

00:01:36.823 --> 00:01:40.217
the Patriots honed in on the
specific intelligence failure

00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:42.235
that could have prevented
the pick from happening

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:43.516
in the first place.

00:01:43.516 --> 00:01:44.947
Not that they were nitpicking

00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:46.679
or indulging in perfectionism,

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:50.953
they had higher standards
of performance to adhere to.

00:01:50.953 --> 00:01:52.453
For years, Scott Pioli,

00:01:52.453 --> 00:01:54.270
Director of Personnel for the Patriots,

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.138
kept the photo on his
desk of Dave Stachelski,

00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:59.833
a player the team had
drafted in the fifth round

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:01.893
but who never made it
through training camp.

00:02:01.893 --> 00:02:03.307
It was a reminder,

00:02:03.307 --> 00:02:05.679
you're not as good as you think.

00:02:05.679 --> 00:02:07.738
You don't have it all figured out.

00:02:07.738 --> 00:02:08.877
Stay focused.

00:02:08.877 --> 00:02:10.524
Do better.

00:02:10.524 --> 00:02:13.036
Bo Jackson wouldn't get
impressed when he hit a home run

00:02:13.036 --> 00:02:14.168
or ran a touchdown

00:02:14.168 --> 00:02:17.094
because he knew he
hadn't done it perfectly.

00:02:17.094 --> 00:02:18.639
In fact, he didn't ask for the ball

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:20.706
after his first hit in
Major League Baseball

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:21.871
for that reason.

00:02:21.871 --> 00:02:25.053
To him, it was just a
ground ball up the middle.

00:02:25.053 --> 00:02:28.858
This is a characteristic
of how great people think.

00:02:28.858 --> 00:02:31.198
It's not that they find
failure in every success.

00:02:31.198 --> 00:02:33.784
They just hold themselves
to a standard that exceeds

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:36.916
what society might consider
to be objective success.

00:02:36.916 --> 00:02:38.014
Because of that,

00:02:38.014 --> 00:02:40.176
they don't much care
what other people think.

00:02:40.176 --> 00:02:42.823
They care whether they
meet their own standards

00:02:42.823 --> 00:02:45.471
and these standards are much, much higher

00:02:45.471 --> 00:02:47.085
than everyone else's.

00:02:47.085 --> 00:02:48.360
The Patriots saw the Brady pick

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.397
as being more lucky than smart

00:02:50.397 --> 00:02:51.790
and although some people are fine

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.602
giving themselves credit for luck,

00:02:53.602 --> 00:02:55.125
they weren't.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:56.235
In this instance,

00:02:56.235 --> 00:02:58.831
instead of celebrating or
congratulating themselves,

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:00.423
they put their heads back down

00:03:00.423 --> 00:03:03.770
and focus on how to get even better.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.018
This isn't necessarily fun by the way.

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:09.006
It can feel like self-inflicted
torture sometimes,

00:03:09.006 --> 00:03:11.759
but it does force you to always keep going

00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:13.514
and always improve.

00:03:13.514 --> 00:03:16.715
For us, the scoreboard can't
be the only scoreboard.

00:03:16.715 --> 00:03:18.549
Warren Buffett has said the same thing.

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:20.933
Making the distinction
between the inner scorecard

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:22.410
and the external one,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.340
your potential, the absolute
best you're capable of,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.552
that's the metric to
measure yourself against,

00:03:27.552 --> 00:03:29.135
your standards are.

00:03:30.032 --> 00:03:31.601
Winning is not enough.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:32.951
Anyone can win,

00:03:32.951 --> 00:03:36.112
but not everyone can be
the best they can be.

00:03:36.112 --> 00:03:38.365
Think of the times that you've
excused your own behavior

00:03:38.365 --> 00:03:40.594
with no one will know.

00:03:40.594 --> 00:03:42.824
Because it's not about
what you can get away with,

00:03:42.824 --> 00:03:45.446
it's about what you
should or shouldn't do.

00:03:45.446 --> 00:03:48.546
A person who judges themselves
based on his own standards

00:03:48.546 --> 00:03:49.768
doesn't crave the spotlight

00:03:49.768 --> 00:03:53.246
the same way as someone who
lets applause dictate success.

00:03:53.246 --> 00:03:55.201
A person who can think longterm

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:58.110
doesn't pity herself during
her short-term setbacks.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.853
A person who values the
team can share credit

00:04:00.853 --> 00:04:02.472
and subsume his own interest

00:04:02.472 --> 00:04:04.692
in a way that most others can't.

00:04:04.692 --> 00:04:07.411
Reflecting on what went
well or how amazing we are

00:04:07.411 --> 00:04:08.842
doesn't get us anywhere

00:04:08.842 --> 00:04:11.361
except maybe to where we are right now,

00:04:11.361 --> 00:04:12.935
but we want to go further.

00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:14.118
We want more.

00:04:14.118 --> 00:04:16.701
We want to continue to improve.

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:22.035
This is a characteristic
of how great people think.

